  <entry>
    <title>A tour of barkdown</title>
    <id>https://pratik.deoghare.com/barkdown/posts/tour/</id>
    <published>2026-11-03T00:00:00+01:00</published>
    <updated>2026-11-03T00:00:00+01:00</updated>
    <link href="https://pratik.deoghare.com/barkdown/posts/tour/" rel="alternate" type="text/html"></link>
    <category term="barkdown"></category>
    <category term="brashtag"></category>
    <category term="reference"></category>
    <summary type="text">Every tag barkdown understands, shown in use.</summary>
    <content type="html" xml:base="https://pratik.deoghare.com/barkdown/posts/tour/">&lt;div class=&#34;epigraph&#34;&gt;&lt;blockquote&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Programs must be written for people to read, and only incidentally for machines to execute.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;footer class=&#34;by&#34;&gt;— Harold Abelson and Gerald Jay Sussman, &lt;cite&gt;Structure and Interpretation of Computer Programs&lt;/cite&gt;&lt;/footer&gt;&#xA;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;newthought&#34;&gt;This post shows&lt;/span&gt; what a barkdown post can contain. Its source is &lt;code&gt;posts/2026-11-03-tour/index.bt&lt;/code&gt;;&#xA;read the two side by side.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;nav class=&#34;toc&#34; id=&#34;contents&#34; aria-label=&#34;In this post&#34;&gt;&lt;p class=&#34;toc-title&#34;&gt;In this post&lt;/p&gt;&lt;ul&gt;&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&#34;#text&#34;&gt;Text&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&#34;#lists&#34;&gt;Lists&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&#34;#tables&#34;&gt;Tables&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&#34;#code&#34;&gt;Code&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&#34;#notes-and-asides&#34;&gt;Notes and asides&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&#34;#figures&#34;&gt;Figures&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&#34;#math-and-diagrams&#34;&gt;Math and diagrams&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&#34;#raw-html&#34;&gt;Raw HTML&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;&lt;/nav&gt;&lt;a class=&#34;goto-toc&#34; href=&#34;#contents&#34;&gt;Back to contents&lt;/a&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;text&#34;&gt;Text &lt;a class=&#34;anchor&#34; href=&#34;#text&#34; aria-label=&#34;Link to this section&#34;&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Paragraphs are separated by blank lines. Inside them you can write &lt;b&gt;bold&lt;/b&gt;, &lt;i&gt;italic&lt;/i&gt;, &lt;em&gt;emphasis&lt;/em&gt;,&#xA;&lt;code&gt;inline code&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;inline code&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;mark&gt;highlights&lt;/mark&gt;, &lt;s&gt;struck text&lt;/s&gt;, H&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;O, E = mc&lt;sup&gt;2&lt;/sup&gt;, keys&#xA;like &lt;kbd&gt;Ctrl&lt;/kbd&gt; + &lt;kbd&gt;C&lt;/kbd&gt;, and abbreviations such as &lt;abbr title=&#34;HyperText Markup Language&#34;&gt;HTML&lt;/abbr&gt;. A line&#xA;break&lt;br&gt;goes like this. The characters &lt;code&gt;#&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;{&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;}&lt;/code&gt; and backticks go between backticks.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Links take the URL first: &lt;a href=&#34;https://github.com/PratikDeoghare/brashtag&#34; class=&#34;external&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;&gt;the brashtag repository&lt;/a&gt;.&#xA;Links to other posts use their slug, like &lt;a href=&#34;/barkdown/posts/welcome/&#34;&gt;Welcome to barkdown&lt;/a&gt;, and are checked when the site is built. A URL with&#xA;a fragment goes between backticks: &lt;a href=&#34;https://go.dev/doc/#learning&#34; class=&#34;external&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;&gt;learning Go&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;blockquote&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Let us change our traditional attitude to the construction of programs: Instead of imagining that our main&#xA;  task is to instruct a computer what to do, let us concentrate rather on explaining to human beings what we&#xA;  want a computer to do.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;footer class=&#34;by&#34;&gt;— Donald E. Knuth, &lt;cite&gt;Literate Programming&lt;/cite&gt;&lt;/footer&gt;&#xA;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;lists&#34;&gt;Lists &lt;a class=&#34;anchor&#34; href=&#34;#lists&#34; aria-label=&#34;Link to this section&#34;&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Each line is an item.&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;A line may start with a dash, as in Markdown.&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;A nested list sits on a line of its own.&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Items can hold &lt;b&gt;markup&lt;/b&gt; and &lt;code&gt;code, with commas&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;ol&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;First&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Second&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;Third&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ol&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The comma separated list of the original barkdown still works, and like every list it is a block of its own:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;one&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;two&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;li&gt;three&lt;/li&gt;&#xA;&lt;/ul&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;tables&#34;&gt;Tables &lt;a class=&#34;anchor&#34; href=&#34;#tables&#34; aria-label=&#34;Link to this section&#34;&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;table&#34;&gt;&lt;table&gt;&lt;caption&gt;Cells are separated by pipes; a row of dashes makes the row above it a header.&lt;/caption&gt;&lt;thead&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;th&gt;Language&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th&gt;Typing&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th&gt;First appeared&lt;/th&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&#xA;&lt;/thead&gt;&lt;tbody&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt;Go&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;static&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;2009&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&#xA;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt;Python&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;dynamic&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;1991&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&#xA;&lt;/tbody&gt;&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;code&#34;&gt;Code &lt;a class=&#34;anchor&#34; href=&#34;#code&#34; aria-label=&#34;Link to this section&#34;&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;codeblock&#34;&gt;&lt;div class=&#34;code-head&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;lang&#34;&gt;go&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-go&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;tok-k&#34;&gt;package&lt;/span&gt; main&#xA;&#xA;&lt;span class=&#34;tok-k&#34;&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;tok-s&#34;&gt;&amp;#34;fmt&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;span class=&#34;tok-c&#34;&gt;// main says hello.&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;span class=&#34;tok-k&#34;&gt;func&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;tok-f&#34;&gt;main&lt;/span&gt;() {&#xA;&#x9;fmt.&lt;span class=&#34;tok-f&#34;&gt;Println&lt;/span&gt;(&lt;span class=&#34;tok-s&#34;&gt;&amp;#34;Hello, barkdown&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;, &lt;span class=&#34;tok-n&#34;&gt;42&lt;/span&gt;)&#xA;}&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;codeblock&#34;&gt;&lt;div class=&#34;code-head&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;lang&#34;&gt;python&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-python&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;tok-k&#34;&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;tok-f&#34;&gt;fib&lt;/span&gt;(n):&#xA;    &lt;span class=&#34;tok-s&#34;&gt;&amp;#34;&amp;#34;&amp;#34;Return the n-th Fibonacci number.&amp;#34;&amp;#34;&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;    a, b = &lt;span class=&#34;tok-n&#34;&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;, &lt;span class=&#34;tok-n&#34;&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;    &lt;span class=&#34;tok-k&#34;&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; _ &lt;span class=&#34;tok-k&#34;&gt;in&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;tok-b&#34;&gt;range&lt;/span&gt;(n):&#xA;        a, b = b, a + b&#xA;    &lt;span class=&#34;tok-k&#34;&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; a&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;codeblock&#34;&gt;&lt;div class=&#34;code-head&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;lang&#34;&gt;sh&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-sh&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;tok-c&#34;&gt;# build the site and publish it&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;barkdown build -strict &amp;amp;&amp;amp; rsync -a public/ &lt;span class=&#34;tok-s&#34;&gt;&amp;#34;$HOST:/var/www/site/&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;codeblock&#34;&gt;&lt;div class=&#34;code-head&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;lang&#34;&gt;bt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-bt&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;tok-k&#34;&gt;#title{&lt;/span&gt;A post&lt;span class=&#34;tok-k&#34;&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;Some text with &lt;span class=&#34;tok-s&#34;&gt;`#code`&lt;/span&gt; and a &lt;span class=&#34;tok-k&#34;&gt;#b{&lt;/span&gt;bold&lt;span class=&#34;tok-k&#34;&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; word.&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;notes-and-asides&#34;&gt;Notes and asides &lt;a class=&#34;anchor&#34; href=&#34;#notes-and-asides&#34; aria-label=&#34;Link to this section&#34;&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Notes are written in place&lt;label for=&#34;sn-1&#34; class=&#34;margin-toggle sidenote-number&#34;&gt;1&lt;/label&gt;&lt;input type=&#34;checkbox&#34; id=&#34;sn-1&#34; class=&#34;margin-toggle&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;sidenote&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;sidenote-mark&#34;&gt;1&lt;/span&gt; Like this one. It sits in the margin, level with the line that mentions it.&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;and numbered automatically&lt;label for=&#34;sn-2&#34; class=&#34;margin-toggle sidenote-number&#34;&gt;2&lt;/label&gt;&lt;input type=&#34;checkbox&#34; id=&#34;sn-2&#34; class=&#34;margin-toggle&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;sidenote&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;sidenote-mark&#34;&gt;2&lt;/span&gt; On a phone there is no margin, so the number becomes a button: tap it to read the&#xA;note.&lt;/span&gt;. A margin note has no number.&lt;label for=&#34;mn-1&#34; class=&#34;margin-toggle&#34; aria-label=&#34;Show note&#34;&gt;&amp;#8853;&lt;/label&gt;&lt;input type=&#34;checkbox&#34; id=&#34;mn-1&#34; class=&#34;margin-toggle&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;marginnote&#34;&gt;A margin note is for a remark or a caption that needs no&#xA;reference mark.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;newthought&#34;&gt;A change of subject&lt;/span&gt; that does not deserve a heading can open with small capitals instead.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;aside class=&#34;callout callout-note&#34;&gt;&lt;p class=&#34;callout-title&#34;&gt;Note&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Call-outs come in six kinds: note, tip, info, warning, danger and important.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;/aside&gt;&#xA;&lt;aside class=&#34;callout callout-tip&#34;&gt;&lt;p class=&#34;callout-title&#34;&gt;A custom title&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;A &lt;code&gt;title&lt;/code&gt; bag inside a call-out replaces its label.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;/aside&gt;&#xA;&lt;aside class=&#34;callout callout-warning&#34;&gt;&lt;p class=&#34;callout-title&#34;&gt;Warning&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;A stray &lt;code&gt;}&lt;/code&gt; in prose ends the document early. Barkdown reports the line.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;/aside&gt;&#xA;&lt;details&gt;&lt;summary&gt;Click to see more&lt;/summary&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;This stays hidden until the reader asks for it.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;/details&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;figures&#34;&gt;Figures &lt;a class=&#34;anchor&#34; href=&#34;#figures&#34; aria-label=&#34;Link to this section&#34;&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;figure&gt;&lt;img src=&#34;diagram.svg&#34; alt=&#34;How barkdown works: a post is parsed into a tree, and the tree is rendered as HTML. The image lives next to the post, in its bundle.&#34; loading=&#34;lazy&#34;&gt;&lt;figcaption&gt;How barkdown works: a post is parsed into a tree, and the tree is rendered as HTML. The&#xA;image lives next to the post, in its bundle.&lt;/figcaption&gt;&lt;/figure&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;math-and-diagrams&#34;&gt;Math and diagrams &lt;a class=&#34;anchor&#34; href=&#34;#math-and-diagrams&#34; aria-label=&#34;Link to this section&#34;&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Inline math is code between dollar signs: &lt;span class=&#34;math&#34;&gt;\(e^{i\pi} + 1 = 0\)&lt;/span&gt;. Display math uses a bag:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;math&#34;&gt;\[\sum_{k=1}^{n} k = \frac{n(n+1)}{2}\]&lt;/div&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Diagrams are Mermaid code blocks:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;pre class=&#34;mermaid&#34;&gt;graph LR&#xA;  A[post.bt] --&amp;gt; B[brashtag tree] --&amp;gt; C[HTML page]&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;raw-html&#34;&gt;Raw HTML &lt;a class=&#34;anchor&#34; href=&#34;#raw-html&#34; aria-label=&#34;Link to this section&#34;&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p class=&#34;center&#34;&gt;&lt;small&gt;When nothing else will do, HTML passes through.&lt;/small&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;</content>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <title>Notes on margins</title>
    <id>https://pratik.deoghare.com/barkdown/posts/margins/</id>
    <published>2026-11-02T00:00:00+01:00</published>
    <updated>2026-11-09T07:45:00+01:00</updated>
    <link href="https://pratik.deoghare.com/barkdown/posts/margins/" rel="alternate" type="text/html"></link>
    <category term="design"></category>
    <category term="tufte"></category>
    <summary type="text">Thoughts on margins as they come to me while building this site&#39;s theme. They will keep arriving. Tufte&#39;s books keep a wide margin on every page, and use it: notes,…</summary>
    <content type="html" xml:base="https://pratik.deoghare.com/barkdown/posts/margins/">&lt;p&gt;Thoughts on margins as they come to me while building this site&amp;#39;s theme. They will keep arriving.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;section class=&#34;timeline&#34; data-order=&#34;newest&#34;&gt;&#xA;&lt;p class=&#34;timeline-head&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;4 thoughts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;article class=&#34;thought&#34; id=&#34;t-2026-11-09-0745&#34;&gt;&#xA;&lt;p class=&#34;thought-stamp&#34;&gt;&lt;a href=&#34;#t-2026-11-09-0745&#34;&gt;&lt;time datetime=&#34;2026-11-09T07:45:00+01:00&#34;&gt;9 November 2026, 07:45&lt;/time&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;On a phone there is no margin, so the notes fold away behind their numbers. Tufte CSS does this with a&#xA;  hidden checkbox and no script at all, which is the kind of trick worth keeping.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;/article&gt;&#xA;&lt;article class=&#34;thought&#34; id=&#34;t-2026-11-06-2210&#34;&gt;&#xA;&lt;p class=&#34;thought-stamp&#34;&gt;&lt;a href=&#34;#t-2026-11-06-2210&#34;&gt;&lt;time datetime=&#34;2026-11-06T22:10:00+01:00&#34;&gt;6 November 2026, 22:10&lt;/time&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Code wants a margin too: the language of a listing, its name in a literate program, a button to copy it.&#xA;  None of these is part of the code, so none of them should sit on top of it.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;/article&gt;&#xA;&lt;article class=&#34;thought&#34; id=&#34;t-2026-11-04&#34;&gt;&#xA;&lt;p class=&#34;thought-stamp&#34;&gt;&lt;a href=&#34;#t-2026-11-04&#34;&gt;&lt;time datetime=&#34;2026-11-04T00:00:00+01:00&#34;&gt;4 November 2026&lt;/time&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;A footnote asks the reader to leave the line and come back. A sidenote waits beside it.&lt;label for=&#34;sn-1&#34; class=&#34;margin-toggle sidenote-number&#34;&gt;1&lt;/label&gt;&lt;input type=&#34;checkbox&#34; id=&#34;sn-1&#34; class=&#34;margin-toggle&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;sidenote&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;sidenote-mark&#34;&gt;1&lt;/span&gt; This one,&#xA;  for instance.&lt;/span&gt; The difference sounds small and is not.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;/article&gt;&#xA;&lt;article class=&#34;thought&#34; id=&#34;t-2026-11-02-0815&#34;&gt;&#xA;&lt;p class=&#34;thought-stamp&#34;&gt;&lt;a href=&#34;#t-2026-11-02-0815&#34;&gt;&lt;time datetime=&#34;2026-11-02T08:15:00+01:00&#34;&gt;2 November 2026, 08:15&lt;/time&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Tufte&amp;#39;s books keep a wide margin on every page, and use it: notes, small pictures, captions. The margin&#xA;  is not empty space waiting for something; it is where the second voice of the book lives.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;/article&gt;&#xA;&lt;/section&gt;&#xA;</content>
  </entry>
